1
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:08,069
Self-driving laboratories, or SDLs,

2
00:00:08,069 --> 00:00:13,035
automate experiments and use the results to choose what to test next.

3
00:00:13,315 --> 00:00:19,874
Language-model agents such as Coscientist and ACRA have already run robotic experiments.

4
00:00:20,154 --> 00:00:25,930
Worldwide — from Canada's Acceleration Consortium to the US Genesis Mission and

5
00:00:25,930 --> 00:00:32,219
Japan's ARiSE programme — efforts are under way to build and scale SDL infrastructure.

6
00:00:32,919 --> 00:00:38,976
Cloud laboratories already let researchers share instruments remotely, without owning a lab.

7
00:00:39,256 --> 00:00:45,396
As this grows, a single facility could run many experiments at once — hundreds of instruments,

8
00:00:45,396 --> 00:00:51,013
nearly a hundred robot arms, and a fleet of mobile robots moving samples between them.

9
00:00:51,713 --> 00:00:54,791
But scale doesn't make resources unlimited.

10
00:00:55,071 --> 00:00:58,975
Instruments, robot arms, floor space and time are all finite,

11
00:00:58,975 --> 00:01:02,879
and experiments compete for the same equipment and transport.

12
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:09,438
Every lab needs a scheduler and resource manager — deciding what to install, and what to run first.

13
00:01:10,138 --> 00:01:13,712
How do we know whether that decision-maker is any good?

14
00:01:13,992 --> 00:01:20,570
We built LabOpsBench: a benchmark that runs an agent's decisions inside a simulated laboratory,

15
00:01:20,570 --> 00:01:25,486
with instruments, robot arms, mobile robots, queues and finite buffers.

16
00:01:25,766 --> 00:01:28,619
It simulates operations — not chemistry.

17
00:01:29,419 --> 00:01:34,774
At time zero, the agent sees every unfinished job — including future arrivals,

18
00:01:34,774 --> 00:01:39,924
with dependencies and durations — plus the room layout and resource limits.

19
00:01:40,204 --> 00:01:45,969
It picks an equipment configuration from shared candidates and gives each job a priority.

20
00:01:46,249 --> 00:01:51,505
The engine validates the decision, dispatches operations and moves the robots.

21
00:01:51,785 --> 00:01:54,051
Every 1,800 simulated seconds,

22
00:01:54,051 --> 00:01:59,337
the agent can re-prioritise or request a permitted equipment exchange.

23
00:01:59,617 --> 00:02:03,784
The score is makespan — time until the last sample is unloaded.

24
00:02:04,064 --> 00:02:06,349
Unfinished runs get no score.

25
00:02:07,249 --> 00:02:11,494
To ground the workloads, we turned public LeMat-Synth annotations —

26
00:02:11,494 --> 00:02:16,879
synthesis procedures extracted from the literature — into eight scheduling templates.

27
00:02:17,159 --> 00:02:25,396
They power fourteen scenarios, from small cells to a hall with 32 instruments, 32 arms and 64 jobs.

28
00:02:26,396 --> 00:02:33,159
Here are two agents on the same task: twelve jobs — six five-step chains and six single mixing jobs.

29
00:02:33,439 --> 00:02:37,554
GPT-6 Luna's plan: feed the oven pipeline first.

30
00:02:37,834 --> 00:02:42,830
It ranks the long chains on top, keeps the layout, and pushes those chains through.

31
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:47,206
Jev gets exactly the same observation and candidates.

32
00:02:47,486 --> 00:02:53,022
Instead of a fixed order, it keeps revising its ranking across seventeen decisions,

33
00:02:53,022 --> 00:02:55,957
mixing the two job types as the lab changes.

34
00:02:56,237 --> 00:02:59,038
Same lab, same jobs — different strategies.

35
00:03:00,338 --> 00:03:04,028
We also built a web interface for every recorded run.

36
00:03:04,308 --> 00:03:12,791
On this task, Luna finished in 8.27 hours, Jev in 8.33, and first-in-first-out took 12.6.

37
00:03:13,071 --> 00:03:17,020
The best method changes by task: on compact lab layouts,

38
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:20,193
Jev and a workload-aware algorithm beat Luna.

39
00:03:20,473 --> 00:03:25,696
Click any result to replay it in 2D or 3D and step through each decision.

40
00:03:25,976 --> 00:03:30,765
Playback only reads recorded engine events — it never changes the score.

41
00:03:31,465 --> 00:03:33,677
Today, LabOpsBench scores time.

42
00:03:33,957 --> 00:03:37,902
But future labs will need to weigh much more — scarce elements,

43
00:03:37,902 --> 00:03:40,782
electricity, water — in one combined decision.

44
00:03:41,062 --> 00:03:45,112
LabOpsBench is a foundation for evaluating those trade-offs,

45
00:03:45,112 --> 00:03:48,959
and for testing our simulation against real laboratories.
